TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"partial","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou is approximately 5.2° N, not 45.05° N. The latitude comparison and launch-window reasoning therefore rely on an incorrect site coordinate."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The report proposes a 2025 launch using Vega E, while also stating its first launch will occur in 2024. Vega E was not an established available launcher for the proposed schedule; the launch choice and schedule require verification and contingency planning."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"At 600 km altitude, a circular sun-synchronous orbit requires a retrograde inclination near 97.8°. The reported 87.7924° is not sun-synchronous and conflicts with the claim of a retrograde orbit."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN does not indicate the launch facility's location; it specifies the inertial direction of the ascending node at a stated epoch. The orbital elements omit an epoch and use argument of latitude without specifying the reference frame or epoch."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A 600 km orbit does not by itself establish adequate imaging resolution or swath coverage. No instrument resolution, swath, pointing, spectral requirements or coverage analysis is provided to substantiate reef-monitoring performance."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The reported access durations depend on the modeled ground-station coordinates, antenna masks and link assumptions, none of which are supplied. Pass access alone does not establish communications capacity or data downlink sufficiency."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail is proposed but its area, deployment reliability, post-mission orbit lifetime and compliance with disposal guidance are not quantified. A passive sail does not guarantee prompt re-entry from 600 km."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"The report cites Wikipedia, which is explicitly unacceptable under the task instructions; several references are incomplete bibliographic entries."}],"overall":"A coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept is presented with substantial mission-segment coverage and STK access outputs. Important orbital and launch-site errors undermine the design’s central claims, while users, disposal performance and supporting files are insufficiently documented. Citation and editing weaknesses also limit report quality.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose and Australian conservation rationale are explained, and a single spacecraft is justified in terms of coverage needs, simplicity and cost. The operator and customer groups are not clearly identified, and the rationale and user discussion are not supported consistently by formal literature citations.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The spacecraft is intended to image and send data to ground stations for collation and analysis."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit, STK access analysis, ground station, duration and disposal, with useful simulation outputs. However, the stated inclination is inconsistent with a 600 km sun-synchronous orbit: it should be about 97.8°, not 87.79°. The launch-site latitude is also wrong, launch timing is inconsistent, and the maneuver/EOL discussion lacks a quantified disposal plan. No software files are supplied.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 87.7924^{\\circ} $"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the proposed launcher, orbit and ground station and notes launch uncertainty. It is relevant but mostly restates design choices, does not assess key limitations or uncertainties in the evidence, and repeats the incorrect characterization of the orbit as suitable for consistent illumination.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, executive summary, figures, tables and bibliography. However, grammar and editing problems remain, the EOL sentence is incomplete, and figure/table presentation and citations are inconsistent. The bibliography is mostly bare URLs and includes Wikipedia, which the task explicitly disallows. These issues reduce professionalism and traceability.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes"}]}]}
